    {
      "id": "ES-C03-me",
      "sequence": 130,
      "title": "me — your first pronoun, and a real English twin",
      "headword": "me",
      "romanization": "me",
      "gloss": "me / myself",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:27e54c87738449da",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A tiny word doing big work. me is your first pronoun — and unlike hola, its English look-alike is the real thing: a true cousin, not a coincidence."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "vowel-e — one clean syllable, meh (the e of bed)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "me — \"me / myself.\" Root: Latin me (the object form of ego, \"I\")."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Here's the satisfying part: English me is a genuine cousin, not a false friend. Spanish me comes from Latin me; English me comes from Proto-Germanic — but both trace back to the same ancient ancestor (the Proto-Indo-European root me-, \"me\"). So do English my and mine. This is the opposite of the hola/hello trap you saw earlier: here the resemblance is real, and you can lean on it hard. Spanish me = English me, all the way down."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: what a pronoun is",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A pronoun is a stand-in word that points to a person or thing without naming them — I, you, he, me, it. me specifically is an object pronoun: it marks the person the action lands on (\"call me\", \"give me\"). In Spanish it usually sits right before the verb — you'll see it next lesson in me llamo, literally \"myself I-call.\" For now, just hold me = \"me / myself.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"me\" — meh",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"me\" — *meh*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"me\" then English \"me\", \"my\", \"mine\" — all one family",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"me\" then English \"me\", \"my\", \"mine\" — all one family]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Is Spanish me a true cousin of English me, or a coincidence like hola/hello? (A true cousin — both from the same ancient root.) What part of speech is it? (A pronoun.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C03-llamar",
      "sequence": 140,
      "title": "llamo — \"I call,\" from a root that shouts",
      "headword": "llamo",
      "romanization": "llamo",
      "gloss": "I call (from llamar, to call)",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:b256de03c573917b",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The verb inside \"my name is.\" On its own it means \"to call\" — and its Latin root literally means to shout, which is why it echoes through a whole family of English words."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "me — you'll bolt me onto llamo next lesson."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ll-y — ll sounds like English y: llamo is YA-mo, not LA-mo. see reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "llamo — \"I call.\" The dictionary form (the infinitive) is llamar, \"to call.\" Root: Latin clamare, \"to cry out, to shout.\" That clam- shouts all through English:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "exclaim, exclamation — ex- (\"out\") + clamare: to shout out."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "proclaim, proclamation — pro- (\"forth\"): to call forth."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "acclaim — ad- (\"toward\"): to shout praise toward."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "claim, reclaim, disclaim; and clamor, borrowed whole."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A sound-pattern to bank: Latin cl- became Spanish ll-. clamare becomes llamar. It happened repeatedly: clavem becomes llave (\"key\"), flamma becomes llama (\"flame\"), plovere becomes llover (\"to rain\"). A Spanish word starting ll- is very often a Latin cl-/fl-/pl- word in disguise — and its English cousin usually kept the original cluster (clamor, flame, pluvial)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"llamo\" — YA-mo",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"llamo\" — *YA-mo*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"llamo\" then \"exclaim\", \"proclaim\", \"clamor\" — the shouting root",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"llamo\" then \"exclaim\", \"proclaim\", \"clamor\" — the shouting root]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"llamar\" (to call), \"llave\" (key) — both from Latin cl-",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"llamar\" (to call), \"llave\" (key) — both from Latin *cl-*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does the root clamare mean? (To shout / cry out.) Latin cl- becomes which two letters in Spanish? (ll- — clamare becomes llamar.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C03-me-llamo",
      "sequence": 150,
      "title": "me llamo — \"I call myself\"",
      "headword": "me llamo",
      "romanization": "me llamo",
      "gloss": "my name is (literally \"I call myself\")",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:15d4ae4f57ab020b",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Snap the last two lessons together — me + llamo — and you can introduce yourself. But notice Spanish doesn't say \"my name is\" at all."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "me, llamo."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart — by assembling it",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "me llamo = me (\"myself\") + llamo (\"I call\") = literally \"I call myself.\" Then the name: Me llamo Susana — \"I call myself Susan.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: reflexive verbs",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "When the action loops back onto the person doing it — you call yourself — the verb is reflexive, and Spanish marks it with that little pronoun in front: me (\"myself\"). llamo alone is \"I call [someone else]\"; me llamo is \"I call myself.\" English can do this (\"I call myself Sam\") but usually doesn't bother, flattening it to \"my name is.\" Spanish keeps the reflexive loop visible."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "This matters because the pronoun changes with the person: me llamo (I call myself) becomes te llamas (you-informal call yourself) becomes se llama (he/she/you-formal calls himself). You'll meet te and se in a few lessons; for now, lock in me llamo for yourself."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "culture-pragmatics",
          "title": "Why it's said this way",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Spanish presents your name not as a thing you have (\"my name is\") but as what you do — what you call yourself. It's subtly more active, and it's the completely standard, neutral way to introduce yourself."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"me llamo\" + your own name",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"me llamo\" + your own name]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"hola, me llamo ___\" — greeting + introduction",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"hola, me llamo ___\" — greeting + introduction]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "what does me llamo literally mean, word for word?",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: what does *me llamo* literally mean, word for word?]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Literally, what is me llamo? (\"I call myself.\") What makes it reflexive? (The me — the action loops back on you.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
